<p>Each vehicle class demands specific adjustments in loading, securing, and protection strategies to withstand the vibrations and movements inherent in long-distance road travel. Understanding these differences allows logistics providers to deploy the right equipment and techniques for every shipment.</p>
<h3>Category-Specific Handling Strategies</h3>
<p>For compact hatchbacks and standard sedans, the primary focus is on efficient space utilization and secure attachment. These vehicles typically feature lower ground clearance and lighter chassis weights. Handlers must use standard wheel chocks and ratchet straps to prevent shifting.</p>
<p>However, the lower profile makes them susceptible to impacts from debris or other vehicles on the carrier. Consequently, applying soft padding along the lower rocker panels and wheel arches is critical. Weight distribution should be balanced evenly across the axle points to prevent sagging, ensuring the vehicle remains stable during braking and acceleration phases.</p>
<p>SUVs present a different challenge due to their higher center of gravity and increased mass. The taller structure amplifies the risk of tipping or excessive swaying on uneven roads. Custom cradles with reinforced base plates are necessary to support the heavier frame without damaging the undercarriage.</p>
<p style="font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 12px;">Padding layers must be thicker, particularly around the roof pillars and side mirrors, which are more exposed to the elements.</p>
